@page
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Blog | Harsh Gupta - Production Systems, Architecture & Security";
}
<meta name="description" content="Engineering insights on production systems, architecture decisions, security vulnerabilities, and operational excellence from a senior architect with 12+ years experience.">
<meta property="og:title" content="Blog - Harsh Gupta">
<meta property="og:description" content="In-depth articles on .NET architecture, production failures, security vulnerabilities, and system design patterns written for senior engineers and architects.">
<meta property="og:type" content="website">
<meta name="twitter:label1" value="Articles">
<meta name="twitter:data1" value="4 Published">

<script type="application/ld+json">
{
  "@@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@@type": "CollectionPage",
  "name": "Blog",
  "description": "Articles on production systems, architecture, security, and operations",
  "url": "https://harshgupta.dev/Blog",
  "mainEntity": {
    "@@type": "ItemList",
    "itemListElement": [
      {
        "@@type": "BlogPosting",
        "position": 1,
        "url": "https://harshgupta.dev/Blog/AdoNetVsEntityFramework",
        "headline": "When NOT to Use Entity Framework (And Why I Chose ADO.NET)",
        "description": "Strategic guide to choosing between Entity Framework and ADO.NET for production systems.",
        "datePublished": "2025-01-10",
        "author": {"@@type": "Person", "name": "Harsh Gupta"}
      },
      {
        "@@type": "BlogPosting",
        "position": 2,
        "url": "https://harshgupta.dev/Blog/RareProductionFailures",
        "headline": "The 0.01% Bug: Why Rare Production Failures Are the Hardest to Fix",
        "description": "How statistically insignificant failures destroy user trust and design patterns that surface invisible bugs.",
        "datePublished": "2025-01-28",
        "author": {"@@type": "Person", "name": "Harsh Gupta"}
      },
      {
        "@@type": "BlogPosting",
        "position": 3,
        "url": "https://harshgupta.dev/Blog/SecurityInProduction",
        "headline": "Security Looks Clean on Paper. Production Makes It Ugly.",
        "description": "How security architecture breaks under real user behavior and distributed state.",
        "datePublished": "2025-02-15",
        "author": {"@@type": "Person", "name": "Harsh Gupta"}
      },
      {
        "@@type": "BlogPosting",
        "position": 4,
        "url": "https://harshgupta.dev/Blog/WhatBreaksInProduction",
        "headline": "What Actually Breaks in Production (and Why You Never See It in Dev)",
        "description": "Real production failures from connection pools to caching race conditions.",
        "datePublished": "2025-03-12",
        "author": {"@@type": "Person", "name": "Harsh Gupta"}
      }
    ]
  }
}
</script>

<section style="padding-top: 150px; min-height: 80vh;">
    <div class="container">
        <span class="section-subtitle reveal">Insights & Articles</span>
        <h2 class="reveal">Blog</h2>
        
        <div class="reveal" style="margin-top: 60px; display: grid; gap: 40px;">
            
            <article class="blog-item">
                <div class="blog-meta">
                    <span class="blog-date">January 10, 2025</span>
                    <span class="blog-category">Architecture</span>
                </div>
                <h3><a href="/Blog/AdoNetVsEntityFramework">When NOT to Use Entity Framework (And Why I Chose ADO.NET)</a></h3>
                <p>Entity Framework is a productivity multiplier—until it isn't. Exploring when explicit SQL control matters more than ORM convenience, and the systems that demand ADO.NET.</p>
            </article>

            <article class="blog-item">
                <div class="blog-meta">
                    <span class="blog-date">January 28, 2025</span>
                    <span class="blog-category">Architecture</span>
                </div>
                <h3><a href="/Blog/RareProductionFailures">The 0.01% Bug: Why Rare Production Failures Are the Hardest to Fix</a></h3>
                <p>How statistically insignificant failures destroy user trust, why dashboards lie, and what it takes to build systems that surface the invisible bugs hiding in production's long tail.</p>
            </article>

            <article class="blog-item">
                <div class="blog-meta">
                    <span class="blog-date">February 15, 2025</span>
                    <span class="blog-category">Security</span>
                </div>
                <h3><a href="/Blog/SecurityInProduction">Security Looks Clean on Paper. Production Makes It Ugly.</a></h3>
                <p>Dual auth models, OTP chains, and UI permissions—how security architecture breaks when it collides with real user behavior, distributed state, and the messy reality of production systems.</p>
            </article>

            <article class="blog-item">
                <div class="blog-meta">
                    <span class="blog-date">March 12, 2025</span>
                    <span class="blog-category">Operations</span>
                </div>
                <h3><a href="/Blog/WhatBreaksInProduction">What Actually Breaks in Production (and Why You Never See It in Dev)</a></h3>
                <p>From connection pool exhaustion to timezone nightmares—exploring the production failures that blindside even experienced teams and how to prevent them.</p>
            </article>

        </div>
    </div>
</section>
